<hr class="top">

<h2 style="margin-top:4px" id="document-control">Document Control<a class="pl" href="#document-control" aria-label="Link to this section">&#182;</a></h2>

<div class="tw"><table>
<tr><th style="width:21%">Document</th><td>Measurement standard proposed for adoption as a rule of general applicability by the authority entered at <span class="jn">J-1</span><span class="jt">adopting authority</span>, under the procedural provision entered at <span class="jn">J-4</span><span class="jt">procedural route</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Proposed rule</th><td>New <span class="jn">J-3</span><span class="jt">rule designation</span>, <i>Measurement and Reporting of Resource Consumption by Large Computational Loads</i>. Designation subject to assignment by the adopting authority; placement proposed adjacent to the large-load interconnection rule entered at <span class="jn">J-7</span><span class="jt">implementing rule</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Standard number</th><td><b>GRUS-STD-60084.</b> This document is that standard, in its uniform edition, and carries the same number for that reason. The Texas instance is GRUS-STD-60084 v2.0, of record in Project No. 60084 and deposited at doi:10.5281/zenodo.23072905. This is GRUS-STD-60084 v2.0-U. The suffix designates the uniform edition and nothing else; it does not designate a different standard, a successor standard, or a revision of the engineering content.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Source</th><td>GRUS-STD-60084 v2.0, <i>The Measurement Standard for Large Computational Loads</i>. This edition converts that document to jurisdiction-neutral form. No substantive position, formula, constant, classification, boundary, method, evidence grade, or definition has been removed, condensed, altered, or withdrawn.</td></tr>
<tr><th>What differs</th><td>This edition differs from GRUS-STD-60084 v2.0 in one respect only. Wherever that document cites a provision of the law of a particular jurisdiction, this edition states a numbered jurisdictional entry in its place, with the governing default and a description of what the entry receives. Every engineering quantity is identical in both documents.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Entries</th><td>Forty-two numbered jurisdictional entries, <span class="jn">J-1</span><span class="jt">adopting authority</span> through <span class="jn">J-42</span><span class="jt">counsel review</span>, scheduled in full below. Each entry carries a stated default. An entry left blank takes its default, and the standard remains operable. No entry reaches any classification, identity, constant, boundary, method, evidence grade, or defined term.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Markup convention</th><td>Text proposed for adoption appears in bordered blocks labeled <b>RULE TEXT PROPOSED FOR ADOPTION</b>. Material outside those blocks is basis and justification and is not proposed for adoption. Where the adopting authority requires additions to be marked, the entire section is new and is marked by underlining in its entirety; no strikethrough appears.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Severability</th><td>Each subsection is drafted to stand independently. The adopting authority may adopt any subsection, or any combination, without adopting the remainder. Subsection (n) so provides. An undeclared jurisdictional entry falls to its default and does not affect any subsection.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Incorporation</th><td>Annexes A through J and Exhibits 1 through 5 are incorporated by reference at subsection (m) and are reproduced in full at the end of this document.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Neutrality</th><td>The measurement specifications use only published, vendor-neutral figures and off-the-shelf instrumentation. No proprietary mechanism is required to comply. No vendor, utility, or verifier holds any exclusive position under the proposed rule. The author seeks no exclusive role.</td></tr>
<tr><th>License</th><td>The author places this document, the proposed rule text, and the incorporated technical appendix in the public domain under the CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication. Adoption requires no license from, no notice to, and no permission of the author, and confers no exclusive right upon the author.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<div class="toc">
<p style="margin:18px 0 6px; font-weight:bold; font-size:11pt; border-bottom:1px solid #000; padding-bottom:3px">Contents</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<tr><td style="width:19%">Schedule</td><td>Schedule of Jurisdictional Entries, <span class="m">J-1</span> through <span class="m">J-42</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Part I</td><td>Purpose and Statutory Frame</td></tr>
<tr><td>Part II</td><td>Why Self-Reporting Is Insufficient as a Matter of Record</td></tr>
<tr><td>Part III</td><td>The Independence-of-Judgment Standard Already in Force</td></tr>
<tr><td>Part IV</td><td>The Classification Framework &mdash; Engineering Basis<br><span class="note">IV.A Hardware sourcing &middot; IV.B Density classes and the air-cooling limit &middot; IV.C The deterministic heat-and-water chain &middot; IV.D Axis 1, workload classes &middot; IV.E Axis 2, cooling classes &middot; IV.F The two-axis matrix &middot; IV.G Uniformity and the socialization gap</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Part V</td><td>Proposed Rule Text, Subsections (a) through (n), with Basis</td></tr>
