<h2 id="how-to-read-this-standard">How to Read This Standard<a class="pl" href="#how-to-read-this-standard" aria-label="Link to this section">&#182;</a></h2>

<p>Parts I through III state the purpose, the statutory frame, and the reason self-reporting cannot satisfy an audit of resource consumption. Part IV supplies the engineering basis for the classification framework &mdash; the two axes against which any facility is read &mdash; stated entirely from published, vendor-neutral figures so that it can be applied by any reviewer without access to proprietary data.</p>

<p>Part V is the proposed rule. Each subsection appears in a bordered block as proposed rule text, followed by its basis in ordinary type. The bordered blocks, taken together and in order, are the complete proposed text of the section. Nothing outside a bordered block is proposed for adoption.</p>

<p>Part VI explains why each subsection is part of the measurement rather than an addition to it. Part VII is the request. The Technical Annexes are incorporated by reference at subsection (m) and are not appendices of convenience. Annex I in particular states every formula used anywhere in this document, together with the worked arithmetic, so that any reviewing engineer can reproduce or refute each number without contacting the author.</p>

<p><b>How the jurisdictional entries work.</b> Everywhere this standard would otherwise cite the law of one jurisdiction, it states a numbered entry instead &mdash; <span class="jn">J-1</span><span class="jt">adopting authority</span> through <span class="jn">J-42</span><span class="jt">counsel review</span> &mdash; followed by a ruled space and, in parentheses, a description of what the space receives. The Schedule immediately below lists every entry once, with its default, so the adopting authority may complete the Schedule in a single pass and need not search the document for the spaces. Completing the Schedule completes the standard. A space left blank takes the default stated for it in the Schedule.</p>

<p>The entries are closed in number and closed in kind. No entry alters a classification on either axis, a measurement boundary, a formula, a physical constant, an instrument accuracy requirement, an evidence grade, or a defined term, for the reason stated at the end of the Schedule: those are not matters of law.</p>

<h2 class="pb" id="schedule-of-jurisdictional-entries">Schedule of Jurisdictional Entries<a class="pl" href="#schedule-of-jurisdictional-entries" aria-label="Link to this section">&#182;</a></h2>

<div class="box">
<p><b>What this Schedule is, in plain terms.</b> This standard is written to be adopted by any jurisdiction. The science in it does not change from one jurisdiction to the next, because the latent heat of vaporization of water does not change at a state line. What changes is the law: which agency holds authority, what minimum water pressure is codified, at how many megawatts the rule attaches, what counts as one service connection.</p>
<p>Rather than write one jurisdiction's statutes into the text and force every other jurisdiction to find and replace them, this standard states a <b>numbered entry</b> at each of those points &mdash; forty-two of them, numbered <span class="m">J-1</span> through <span class="m">J-42</span>. An entry is a labeled blank line with a stated default. The adopting authority writes its own provision on the line. Nothing else in the standard moves.</p>
<p><b>Each entry carries its label wherever it appears.</b> In the body of the document an entry looks like this:</p>
<p style="margin:10px 0 10px 22px"><span class="je"><span class="jn">J-13</span><span class="jt">design pressure</span>&nbsp;<span class="jrl"></span></span></p>
<p>The black tab is the entry number, for cross-reference to this Schedule. The grey tab says what the entry is. The rule is where the citation or value goes. A parenthetical after it, where one appears, states exactly what to write. No reader has to turn back to this page to learn what an entry means.</p>
<p><b>Twenty-two of the forty-two carry a working default</b>, taken from published engineering consensus rather than from any one jurisdiction &mdash; 35 psi, 20 psi, 1.5 gpm per connection, 0.6 gpm, 200 gallons, 75 megawatts, 36 months, and the rest, each shown in the Default column below. <b>A line left blank takes its default, and the standard still operates.</b> Subsection (n) of the rule text says so. That is what separates this Schedule from a form with holes in it.</p>
<p><b>No entry reaches the engineering.</b> Not a classification on either axis, not a measurement boundary, not a formula, not a physical constant, not an instrument accuracy class, not an evidence grade, not a defined term. The closing box of this Schedule lists what is fixed and cannot be varied by any entry on it.</p>
</div>

<p>The adopting authority completes this Schedule. Each entry receives the citation, name, or value indicated in the description column. An entry for which the jurisdiction has no corresponding provision or body is completed <i>none</i>; an entry the jurisdiction adopts at its stated default is completed <i>default adopted</i> or left blank.</p>

<div class="jblk">
<p>Jurisdiction: <span class="jrl"></span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Date: <span class="jrs"></span></p>
<p>Completed by: <span class="jrl"></span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Title: <span class="jr"></span></p>
<p>Body: <span class="jrl" style="min-width:420px"></span></p>
</div>

<h3 id="group-a-the-adopting-authority-and-the-instrument">Group A &mdash; The adopting authority and the instrument<a class="pl" href="#group-a-the-adopting-authority-and-the-instrument" aria-label="Link to this section">&#182;</a></h3>

<div class="tw"><table class="sched">
<tr><th style="width:6%">Entry</th><th style="width:21%">Receives</th><th style="width:30%">Description &mdash; what is entered in the space</th><th style="width:21%">Default</th><th style="width:22%">Value entered</th></tr>
<tr><td class="m c">J-1</td><td>Adopting authority</td><td>The name of the commission, agency, board, or body adopting this standard.</td><td>No default</td><td style="height:26px">&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="m c">J-2</td><td>General rulemaking authority</td><td>The provision conferring general rulemaking authority on the body at <span class="m">J-1</span>.</td><td>No default</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="m c">J-3</td><td>Rule designation</td><td>The code title, chapter, and section number the adopted rule will occupy.</td><td>No default</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="m c">J-4</td><td>Procedural route</td><td>The provision under which a rule of general applicability is proposed, petitioned for, or initiated in this jurisdiction.</td><td>No default</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="m c">J-5</td><td>Large-load interconnection statute</td><td>The statute establishing interconnection standards for large electric loads, including any site-control, disclosure, study, or cost-contribution obligation.</td><td>No default</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="m c">J-6</td><td>Applicability threshold</td><td>The aggregate electrical demand at a single site at which this standard attaches, in megawatts.</td><td><b>75 MW</b></td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="m c">J-7</td><td>Implementing interconnection rule</td><td>The administrative rule implementing the statute at <span class="m">J-5</span>, including any fee or financial-security provision.</td><td>No default</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="m c">J-8</td><td>Interconnection cost-allocation statute</td><td>The provision assigning interconnection and infrastructure cost to the load causing it.</td><td>No default</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
